92186079517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078688 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078688 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078688 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "6c809259-8a13-5c78-bd80-d0d6e8fe9b1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079517 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079517 #edb/ref 1061 486637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079517 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0d627eef-ef65-5238-9901-90c3400ac3c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079517 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079518 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079518 #edb/ref 1061 486638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079518 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "64d8a6fd-3407-5153-8d34-5325bd887b5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079518 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079519 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079519 #edb/ref 1061 486641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079519 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "366dea1e-1586-59cc-a106-0ebd1a1da354" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079519 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079520 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079520 #edb/ref 1061 486644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079520 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4949bbd4-b355-5948-88e4-04282bd52532" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079520 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079521 #edb/ref 1061 486645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079521 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2a86dd84-9a2e-5761-bb0a-6256c5f07a30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079521 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079610 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to express the following series in sigma notation.\n\n$$\nS = \\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}2} + \\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}3} + \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}4} + \\cdots + \\dfrac{\\color{red}19}{\\color{blue}20} + \\dfrac{\\color{red}20}{\\color{blue}21}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we first examine the numerators and denominators of the corresponding sequence.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}2}, \\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}3}, \\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}4}, \\quad \\ldots, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{red}19}{\\color{blue}20}, \\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}20}{\\color{blue}21}\n$$\n\nOur goal is to find a pattern in the terms. To do this, we note the following:\n\n- The numerators form the following sequence:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}1}, \\quad {\\color{red}2}, \\quad {\\color{red}3}, \\quad \\dots, \\quad {\\color{red}19}, \\quad {\\color{red}20}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have $20$ terms in total. The $n$th term of this sequence is given by\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}a_n} = {\\color{red}n}, \\qquad 1\\leq n \\leq 20.\n$$\n\n- The denominators form the following sequence:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2}, \\quad {\\color{blue}3}, \\quad \\dots, \\quad {\\color{blue}20}, \\quad {\\color{blue}21}\n$$\n\nSince the value in the denominator is always *one more than* the numerator, the $n$th term of this sequence is given by\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}b_n} = {\\color{blue}n + 1} , \\qquad 1\\leq n \\leq 20.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $n$th term of the sequence\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}2}, \\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}3}, \\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}4}, \\quad \\ldots, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{red}19}{\\color{blue}20}, \\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}20}{\\color{blue}21}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}a_n}{\\color{blue}b_n} =\\dfrac{\\color{red}n}{\\color{blue}n+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ ranges from $n=1$ to $n=20.$ \n\nTherefore, we can express our series using sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S={\\sum }_{n=1}^{20}\\frac{n}{n+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can check that this answer is correct by writing out the summation in full and checking that we recover the original sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{20}\\frac{n}{n+1} & =\\frac{1}{1+1}+\\frac{2}{2+1}+⋯+\\frac{19}{19+1}+\\frac{20}{20+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{3}+⋯+\\frac{19}{20}+\\frac{20}{21}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079610 #edb/ref 1065 13578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079610 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0c45a9c6-5ee9-5a89-8398-6a3eb37f5f63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079610 #edb/ref 1067 "Expressing Sums in Sigma Notation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079656 #edb/ref 1064 "When writing out a sum, we often have to write a lot of terms. For example, the following sum has five terms:\n\n$$\nS = 2+4+6+8+10\n$$\n\nWe could just as easily encounter a sum with ten terms, or a hundred terms, or a thousand terms, or even more!\n\nThankfully, there is a notation called **sigma notation** that allows us to write sums more compactly, without writing every term.\n\nTo express the given sum $S$ using sigma notation, we first need to come up with the general term $a_n$ in our sum. Here, the $n$th term of our sum has the form $a_n = 2n,$ where $n$ ranges from ${\\color{red}1}$ to ${\\color{blue}5}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2(1)=2 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =2(2)=4 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =2(3)=6 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =2(4)=8 \\\\ {a}_{5} & =2(5)=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our sum can be written as\n\n$$\nS = a_{\\color{red}1} + a_2 + a_3 + a_4 + a_{\\color{blue}5}.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we use the Greek letter $\\displaystyle\\sum$ (called \"sigma\") to denote our summation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S={\\sum }_{n\\,=\\,1}^{5}({a}_{n})={\\sum }_{n\\,=\\,1}^{5}(2n)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe write the $n$th term $({\\color{black}a_n=2n})$ to the right of $\\displaystyle\\sum,$ we put the first index $({\\color{red}1})$ below the sigma as $n={\\color{red}1},$ and we put the last index $({\\color{blue}5})$ above the sigma.\n\nWe can retrieve the expanded sum by substituting all values between $\\color{red}1$ and $\\color{blue}5$ into the $n$th term, and separating each result with a \"$+$\" sign, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}(2n) & =2(1)+2(2)+2(3)+2(4)+2(5) \\\\  & =2+4+6+8+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The sum\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{5} 2n = 2+4+6+8+10\n$$\n\nis an example of a **series**. A series is what we get when we add the terms of a *sequence* together. In this case, we're summing the terms of the sequence $a_n = 2n.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079656 #edb/ref 1065 2837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079656 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "27542f45-7f00-500f-8ef4-8f36f6f8b957" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079656 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549307 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186550032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549307 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186550096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549307 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186550121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549307 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549307 #edb/ref 1045 "Sigma Notation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549307 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186550121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549307 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549307 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549307 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "730efb98-1f2f-5dbd-abfa-6eb2ca40c973" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549373 #edb/ref 1080 "20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549373 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549373 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1d1d49e9-52a3-5714-bf0d-4a751bb9d80a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549423 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{11}(8n+8)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549423 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549423 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "323b99ba-09f2-5bc7-9d64-8e7dd6ab6344" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549458 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{11}8n" #edb/ref 175921860